```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: Wazero embedders who instantiate a WASI module with `NewModuleConfig()` defaults and run a guest that calls `crypto/rand.Reader` (or Rust `getrandom`) for secrets, or uses `context.WithTimeout`/`time.After` for deadlines, without overriding the host RNG/clock knobs — e.g., a CLI tool, test harness, or service that accidentally ships the default config to production.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Real security/availability failure, not a microbenchmark artifact: 30 separate OS processes under DEFAULT produced 100% ed25519 pubkey+token collision (1/30 unique) and a Rust guest produced 10/10 identical keys; control with `WithRandSource(crypto/rand.Reader)` gave 30/30 and 10/10 distinct. Clock decoupling showed 150 ms guest-perceived wait completing in ~4.8 ms real wall (~31× faster), breaking rate-limiters/retries; control with `WithSysWalltime/Nanotime/Nanosleep` gave ~1.0 ratio. The hazard survives any “fast kernel” because it is a host-ABI demotion, not a kernel paging effect.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [Defaults are intentional for deterministic/reproducible testing; per-knob behavior is documented in `config.go` (random_get → fixed-seed, walltime frozen) so maintainers may treat the composition as user misconfiguration rather than a bug; changing defaults breaks existing test reproducibility; no CVE/issue currently owns the composition, so triage may stall; users must opt-in to secure config via `WithRandSource`, `WithSysWalltime`, etc., which is already the documented production path.]
RATIONALE: L0 shows cross-process secret collision (Go: 30/30 identical, control 30/30 distinct; Rust: 10/10 identical, control 10/10 distinct) proving the WASI ABI boundary demotes the gold-standard `crypto/rand` API to a fixed-seed PRNG, and clock decoupling (~31×) silently misfires deadlines — both with controls held and nulls clean. `config.go` documents each knob individually but “NONE warns the composition” that `crypto/rand` is silently demoted. The hazard is ABI-boundary (cross-language) and un-owned, but operational impact is confined to deployments that ship the documented-for-testing default config; production guidance already prescribes overriding knobs, limiting the adopter pool to misconfigured or test-adjacent users.
```
